WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.694
Session management is the mechanism that maintains authenticated state across multiple requests.

2
00:00:05.694 --> 00:00:13.285
HTTP by itself is stateless: one request does not automatically prove anything about a previous request.

3
00:00:13.285 --> 00:00:25.621
After a user signs in, the application needs a reliable way to recognize that same user on later requests without asking for the password every time.

4
00:00:25.621 --> 00:00:35.110
A session provides that continuity, usually through a session identifier, server-side state, a token, or a combination of these patterns.

5
00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:42.227
Authentication, authorization, identity, and session management are related, but they are not the same thing.

6
00:00:42.227 --> 00:00:46.023
Authentication verifies who the user or workload is.

7
00:00:46.023 --> 00:00:50.293
Authorization decides what that identity is allowed to do.

8
00:00:50.293 --> 00:00:56.935
Identity describes attributes such as user ID, role, tenant, account status, or assurance level.

9
00:00:56.935 --> 00:01:02.154
Session management preserves the authenticated state and supporting context across time.

10
00:01:02.154 --> 00:01:11.169
A secure application needs all four concepts to work together rather than assuming that login alone solves access control.

11
00:01:11.169 --> 00:01:24.454
Common architectures include server-side sessions referenced by browser cookies, token-based API sessions, mobile app token flows, and hybrid designs that combine short-lived access tokens with longer-lived refresh mechanisms.

12
00:01:24.454 --> 00:01:35.366
Developers are responsible for selecting appropriate framework features, configuring cookie and token behavior, enforcing lifecycle rules, validating sessions server-side, and logging security-relevant events.

13
00:01:35.366 --> 00:01:39.162
Session management is not just plumbing.

14
00:01:39.162 --> 00:01:44.381
It is the security layer that keeps identity meaningful after login.
